From 1a1f57faab69096a0f20f6cdfdf093f2aeb1c27f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 02:42:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: play progressive 25p and 30p video at its own
 refresh rate

Without a whitelist, Kodi leaves the half refresh rates (25, 29.97 and 30 Hz) out
of the modes it may choose, so 25 fps plays at 50 Hz and 29.97 fps at 59.94 Hz:
interlaced video is not always known as such when playback starts, and a stream
started at 25 Hz would switch the display again once the decoder marks a picture
as interlaced (VideoPlayerVideo then asks for twice the rate).

A stream whose decoder can never mark a picture as interlaced cannot switch again
that way. PROGRESSIVE_VIDEO::Check() decides it from the codec and its parameter
sets, read as FFmpeg 9 reads them, before the first picture:
- H.264: frame_mbs_only_flag set (no field or MBAFF pictures) and no
  pic_struct_present_flag (no picture timing that could signal fields);
- HEVC: neither field_seq_flag nor frame_field_info_present_flag in the VUI of
  every SPS, and no SPS of another layer;
- AV1, VP8, VP9: always, they have no interlaced coding;
- anything else, parameter sets that cannot be read, or an H.264 SPS FFmpeg's
  strict parse would reject (it then retries with other bits): not progressive.
Container flags are not trusted to say progressive.

For such a stream the default whitelist also admits the half refresh rates, so it
gets its exact frame rate: 25p at 25 Hz, 29.97p at 29.97 Hz, 30p at 30 Hz. With a
user whitelist nothing changes: the whitelist decides, as before, so the old
behaviour stays available by whitelisting only the 50/60 Hz modes.

The verdict travels with the stream (CDVDStreamInfo::progressive) to the render
manager through Configure(), as the frame rate does. Within a file it only goes
from progressive to not progressive: a picture marked as interlaced by any
decoder ends it for the rest of the file, and a stream reopened in the middle
(extradata that arrived late, a decoder reopened) cannot make it progressive
again; the next file of a playlist starts over (CRenderManager::NewFile()). Once
a file has its display mode, a mode at twice a corrected frame rate is kept, as
Kodi's default choice did for 25/50 and 29.97/59.94 fps. Native Dolby Vision
keeps the refresh rate chosen here.
